{
  "claims": [
    {
      "claim": "Prior distribution for animal mass has support $\\theta > 0$ and $\\theta \\leq 30$ tons (blue whale mass)",
      "supported": true,
      "evidence_or_problem": "Source: [00:47:09\u201300:48:13] - 'First, they start from prior distribution of possible masses of animals on our planet. So this distribution has restricted support. So of course we understand that mass cannot be negative. So it's a positive semi-axis. And it has some form where, again, there's a theta max because no animal may have mass more than 30 tons. That is the mass of the blue whale.'"
    },
    {
      "claim": "First wisdom (tactile observation) updates posterior distribution $P(\\theta | x)$ using Bayesian inference",
      "supported": true,
      "evidence_or_problem": "Source: [00:49:16\u201300:50:27] - 'he performs Bayesian inference, and he comes up with a posterior distribution. So this is his model, or his likelihood. X is something he observes, times prior distribution, or a normalization constant. And suppose that he touched the elephant's nose. So after touching the nose, he may come up with aha. So this is either nose of some very large animal, or this is a body of some snake-like animal.'"
    },
    {
      "claim": "Second wisdom (acoustic observation) updates posterior distribution $P(\\theta | x, y)$ using Bayesian inference with posterior from first wisdom as new prior",
      "supported": true,
      "evidence_or_problem": "Source: [00:50:56\u201300:52:06] - 'he uses posterior distribution as a new prior distribution. He multiplies it by its own likelihood. He normalizes the result. And he gets new posterior distribution that already reflects the information from two different sources, from first and second with the one.'"
    },
    {
      "claim": "The posterior distribution after multiple observations becomes sharp around a small area",
      "supported": true,
      "evidence_or_problem": "Source: [00:52:43\u201300:53:21] - 'we will probably end up with a posterior distribution that will be pretty sharp around some small area.'"
    },
    {
      "claim": "Bayesian framework allows combining multiple probabilistic models sequentially to build complex inference from simple components",
      "supported": true,
      "evidence_or_problem": "Source: [00:52:43\u201300:53:21] - 'This is one of the advantages of Bayesian framework. We can combine different probabilistic models and we can build a more complicated model from a simple ones like a wall from building blocks.'"
    },
    {
      "claim": "The probabilistic model for tactile observation is $P(x | \\theta)$ where $x$ is tactile feedback",
      "supported": true,
      "evidence_or_problem": "Source: [00:48:44\u201300:49:16] - 'that reflects its observation with possible values of mass of an animal. So this is kind of why with the function. So it shows that if an animal has a mass of theta, how probable is that I feel, what I feel, that I will measure x.'"
    },
    {
      "claim": "The probabilistic model for acoustic observation is $P(y | \\theta)$ where $y$ is acoustic feedback",
      "supported": true,
      "evidence_or_problem": "Source: [00:50:56\u201300:51:26] - 'So Q, Y, given theta. So Y is completely different variable. It lives in completely different dimension. So X reflected the tactile feelings while Y reflects the acoustic feelings.'"
    },
    {
      "claim": "The posterior distribution after two observations is $P(\\theta | x, y) = \\frac{P(y | \\theta) P(\\theta | x)}{P(y)}$",
      "supported": true,
      "evidence_or_problem": "Source: [00:50:56\u201300:52:06] - 'he uses posterior distribution as a new prior distribution. He multiplies it by its own likelihood. He normalizes the result.' (This implies the Bayes' theorem form, though the exact formula is not written out in the speech)"
    }
  ],
  "clarity_1_to_5": 4,
  "caveat": "The transcript mentions 'b model four' which appears to be a misstatement (likely 'beta distribution' or similar), but this is not a claim to evaluate. The notes correctly flag this as unclear."
}
